resources.string:
What helps:
• decide in advance how much and on which days, and write it down;
• at least 2–3 alcohol-free days a week;
• drink slowly, alternate with a glass of water, not on an empty stomach;
• smaller glasses, alcohol-free beer or wine, sparkling water with lemon;
• notice what pushes you (stress, company, evenings) and plan something else for then;
• tell someone close about your goal.

What helps:
• half the plate vegetables;
• vegetables with every main meal;
• fruit as a snack;
• frozen vegetables are just as good and always at hand.

What helps:
• plan a portion instead of a total ban;
• keep sweets out of sight, buy smaller packs;
• swap sugary drinks and juice for water or tea;
• when you crave sweets after a meal: fruit, yogurt or a 10-minute pause.

What helps:
• regular meals during the day, no gaps over 4 hours: the most effective;
• protein at dinner;
• a planned evening snack instead of a spontaneous one;
• the kitchen closes at a set time, then tea or water.

What helps (from cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia):
• get up at the same time every day, weekends too;
• go to bed when sleepy, not because it is time;
• the bed is for sleep only, no phone;
• coffee before noon, fewer screens and less light an hour before bed;
• if you can't sleep for 20 minutes, get up and do something calm.

What helps:
• before eating, name what you feel: hunger, tiredness, sadness, boredom, stress?
• a 10–15 minute pause: a walk, a call, a glass of water; the wave usually passes;
• regular meals during the day, no strict restriction;
• after a slip, no guilt: one evening does not cancel the week.

What helps:
• keep a simple quick home option at hand;
• when eating out, a smaller portion and no sugary drink;
• add vegetables or a salad.
